2.2.m:
Even if the kutapa period falls on both days, one should resort to the lunar day that extends to sunset;
where the proper time for the śrāddha and the like occurs, there the śrāddha is prescribed,
and in a votive observance on an increasing [lunar day], wherever the rising of the sun takes place.

And at the end of the month, one should offer the earthen one at the base of a tree;
under no circumstances should one shake out cloth with another person;
at a parvan śrāddha, one should exclude a temple priest and a widower.

Likewise, in the month of Māgha, that [saptamī] pleases the sun through the previous [day];
through the subsequent [day] it is a manvantara day for rites of bathing, drinking, and the like.
Description of the determination of the best lunar days

in the four directions six squares, three squares, and four squares;
and one square in each of the four corners: this is the determination of the site;
then, excluding Carakī and the rest, forty-five [deities remain];

One should meditate upon the yellow Bhallāṭaka, seated on a lotus, with red ornaments;
and one should conceptualize the god thus: two-armed, with a lotus in his hand.
One should worship with white husked rice using the mantra, “yanmātuḥ”.

Using the mantra, “asya kirīṭa”, with red garlands, sandalwood, and other offerings,
one should worship with supreme devotion. Nīla is declared as the sage;
Triṣṭubh is the meter, and Hrada is proclaimed as the deity.

The mantrin should employ it in the primary rite and also engage it in praise.
The seer of the mantra 'tvannogna...' is named Vāsiṣṭha;
the meter is celebrated as bṛhatī, and the deity is proclaimed to be Fire itself.

(Having meditated on Mahādeva as two-armed, holding a small drum and a trident, accompanied by Umā;
of the mantra *namo vṛkṣebhyaḥ*, Brahmā is the ṛṣi, gāyatrī is the metre, Candra is the deity; the application is for the pleasing of Candra.)
With devotion he should worship the bhūtas all around with fragrant flowers and the like.
